---
// PROPERTY CLEANOUTS — `/property-cleanouts-san-jose/`. Rebuilt following the same gold-standard
// approach as `/hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/` and `/estate-cleanout-san-jose/`
// (docs/30-WEBSITE-MASTER-SPEC.md §20, §20A), with copy reconciled from
// docs/ASEPTACLEAN-SERVICE-LANDING-PAGE-SYSTEM.md §39 (shared with Estate — §39 covers "Estate &
// Property Cleanout" as one landing page) under §20A.1's order: landing copy → map to current
// route → check active/gated state → check claims, pricing and scope → check current copy
// authority → then implement. The existing `propertyCleanoutsPage` record in
// src/data/doc27ServicePages.ts (owner-approved, route-audit-corrected copy) was mined for
// approved language — the hauler boundary, the FAQ answers, the section headings — rather than
// discarded.
//
//   01 SERVICE HERO             photograph + overlay, left-aligned, service confirmed on sight
//   02 WHAT IS STOPPING IT      recognition: triggers that stall a property, "you cannot clean a
//                               floor you cannot reach" — this page's condition-recognition
//                               movement, which Estate deliberately has none of
//   03 STAYS / LEAVES / REVIEW  operational rule system + brief decision-authority note — this
//                               page's signature movement, simpler and less document-like than
//                               Estate's Save List because the audience is not sorting a family's
//                               belongings, it is executing a landlord/owner's approved scope
//   04 PROPERTY GOAL + PLAN     what the property needs to become next, then the clearing
//                               sequence as field procedure — the second mechanism this page runs
//                               on, shared in kind with Estate but different in content (no
//                               family/return-to-family goal; adds "ready to rent/sell/turn over")
//   05 CLEARING + CLEANING      clearing vs cleaning, hidden conditions, why this is not a junk
//                               hauler — the disposal/hauler and basic-option-first content
//   06 EVIDENCE + LOGISTICS     the honest proof gap, phased work, access/logistics — carries more
//                               logistics than Estate per the brief
//   07 FIT + PRICE              qualification + price drivers
//   08 QUESTIONS                seven, the ones that decide the purchase
//   09 CLOSE                    "Clear the property so it can move forward."
//
// THE SPECIALIST THESIS IS §20A.5's, unchanged: for property cleanouts, doc 30 does not name one
// explicitly the way it does for Hoarding and treats Estate/property cleanout as one row — so this
// build adopts the brief's own core-thesis line as the specialist thesis: **Clear the property so
// it can move forward.** This is deliberately NOT Estate's "protect what matters before the
// property gets cleared" (a family-decision argument) and NOT Hoarding's "you stay in control of
// what stays and what leaves" (a condition-management argument). It is a logistics/progression
// argument: the property is stuck, and clearing is what unsticks it.
//
// DISTINCTION FROM ESTATE CLEANOUT, BY DESIGN, NOT BY ACCIDENT:
//   - Estate's signature movement is a Save List — named items and named categories an authorized
//     person defines before clearing starts, framed as protecting a family's irreplaceable things.
//     This page's signature movement is a STAYS / LEAVES / REVIEW rule system framed as an
//     operational scope a landlord, owner or property manager sets so the crew is not guessing —
//     logistics language, not family language. There is no "we do not start by clearing" opening;
//     this page starts with the property's own stalled state.
//   - Estate's second mechanism is the property's end goal, phrased as "sold / rented / returned
//     to family / made usable / emptied" — a family-disposition frame. This page's property-goal
//     list is phrased as "ready to sell / ready to rent / ready for repair / ready for turnover /
//     usable again / emptied" — a next-use/readiness frame with no "returned to family" option,
//     because family disposition is Estate's scope, not this page's.
//   - This page has a condition-recognition movement ("what is stopping the property from moving
//     forward?") that Estate explicitly does not carry — Estate's own build note says "an estate
//     property is not being diagnosed... it is being prepared for its next stage." This page's
//     audience (landlord, owner, investor, property manager) IS being asked to recognize a stalled
//     property, so the movement belongs here.
//   - This page carries more logistics (access, stairs, parking, phased work) and more of the
//     disposal/hauler and junk-hauler-comparison content than Estate, per the brief's explicit
//     instruction that Property Cleanouts "can carry more logistics than Estate." Estate carries
//     the donation/appraisal/remote-family nuance that this page deliberately keeps light.
//   - No save-list document artifact, no "if the family is not in California" remote-decision
//     movement, no donation/appraisal boundary section — those are Estate's estate-specific
//     objections and are not duplicated here. This page's fit-aside instead routes a family/estate
//     situation to Estate Cleanout and a heavy-accumulation situation to Hoarding Cleanup.
// DISTINCTION FROM HOARDING CLEANUP (`/hoarding-cleanup-san-jose/`):
//   Hoarding's signature movement is a five-category decision REGISTER (Keep/Set aside/Donate/
//   Remove/Not sure) applied to an accumulation condition, with a private/dignified tone built
//   around "you stay in control." This page's STAYS/LEAVES/REVIEW system is three categories, not
//   five, framed as a turnover/logistics scope rather than a sensitive personal-condition
//   register, and this page has no condition-recognition movement about hoarding severity — its
//   own condition-recognition movement is about property STATE (vacant, blocked, mid-transition),
//   not accumulation severity.
//
// WHAT THIS REPLACES. The previous build: a 22-line wrapper around the shared
// `ServicePageLayout.astro` template, fed by `propertyCleanoutsPage` in doc27ServicePages.ts —
// the same generic 14-page template that both Hoarding and Estate replaced in their own rebuilds.
// That template's section sequence is identical across all remaining pages with only the record's
// strings changing, which is exactly the "one template, swap the noun" failure mode §20A.5 and
// §20A.25 Q14 rule out.
//
// ROUTE STATE IS UNCHANGED AND DELIBERATELY SO. Same slug, same canonical, same `index, follow`,
// same sitemap membership. The BreadcrumbList trail is unchanged: /property-clearing/ (parent hub)
// through to this page's own H1. SEO title and meta description are rewritten here — see the note
// beside those constants — because the previous template pulled them from
// `propertyCleanoutsPage.seoTitle` / `.metaDescription`, which described a different H1
// ("Property cleanouts in San Jose that keep a vacancy on schedule") than this rebuild uses. New
// title/description describe the same route and the same service, in this page's own words, per
// §20A.22 ("do not create a second page for a keyword variation" — this is one page, one route,
// refreshed copy).
//
// NO CredentialBar ON THIS ROUTE. Same reasoning as Hoarding and Estate: "Insured · COI available
// on request" and "Endorsed · Organic pathogen endorsement" are release-gated by AGENTS.md §3
// pending a current COI match. Taking a gated claim off an indexable page is the conservative
// direction.
//
// NO EMBEDDED FORM. §20A.8's destination rule and §20A.9's "one assessment engine" — CTAs link to
// /request-assessment/?service=property-cleanouts instead of duplicating lead infrastructure.
//
// NO getServiceCityLinks() CALL. `ServiceSlug` in cityFacts.ts is `"hoarding-cleanup" |
// "estate-cleanout"` only — property-cleanouts is not a member, and widening that shared type to
// add a third value affects `cityFacts.ts`, `serviceCityPages.ts`, `serviceCityImplications.ts`
// and the two already-approved pages that read them. That is a shared-data-model change, not a
// single-page content change, so it is out of scope for this pass and is not made here. This page
// carries no "Local planning notes" block as a result — an honest omission, not a broken link.
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import BaseLayout from "@layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import heroImage from "../../assets/aseptaclean/home-property-scope-detail.png";
import { srcsetWidths } from "@components/imageWidths";
import { legal, site } from "@data/site";

// HERO REPOINTED 2026-09-05 to the owner image package, slot 18 `home-property-scope-detail`.
//
// It replaces `condition-development-neglected-kolforn.jpg` (Kolforn, Wikimedia Commons,
// CC BY-SA 4.0), which shipped here as a full-bleed hero with its attribution living only in this
// comment — never on the rendered page. That is the licence exposure recorded in
// docs/06-ASSET-MANIFEST.md, and removing the placement is what closes it for this route.
//
// SUBJECT MATCH. The H1 lede is "when a property has too much left in it to move forward". Slot 18
// shows a furnished room with belongings across a worn floor — a property with contents still in
// it, which is precisely the condition this page addresses. The Commons file it replaces was a
// dated galley kitchen: a neglected-surfaces photograph, not a property-full-of-contents one.
//
// STILL ORIGINAL ILLUSTRATIVE WORK, not Aseptaclean project documentation, not a client property
// and not proof of a completed job. `[ATMOS]`-class under docs/21-CLAIMS-AND-COMPLIANCE-LAW.md §6:
// no crew, no vehicle, no equipment, no work in progress, no before/after pair. Provenance is
// recorded in docs/06-ASSET-MANIFEST.md and nowhere on the page.
//
// Sibling heroes stay distinct: Estate keeps `hoarding-garage-contents` (repointed 2026-09-06,
// see that page's own hero comment) and Hoarding keeps package slot 03, so no two of the three
// cleanout-family pages open on the same photograph.

const canonicalPath = "/property-cleanouts-san-jose/";

// REWRITTEN FROM `propertyCleanoutsPage.seoTitle` / `.metaDescription` (doc27ServicePages.ts),
// which described the page's OLD lead ("Property cleanouts in San Jose that keep a vacancy on
// schedule"). This rebuild's H1 and thesis differ from that lead, so the title/description are
// brought into agreement with what the page now actually says, per §20A.22.
const title = "Property Cleanouts in San Jose | Aseptaclean";
const description =
  "Property cleanouts in San Jose for owners, landlords, property managers and investors. What stays, what leaves and what needs review is agreed before a room is cleared.";

// §20A.8's destination rule + §20A.9's service branch. `?service=property-cleanouts` is read by
// AssessmentForm.astro, added alongside this page (see that component's service-context block).
// It maps to the existing "Landlord turnover" `property_situation` option — already present on
// the form, not invented for this page — and records the real entry route on the lead.
const assessmentHref = "/request-assessment/?service=property-cleanouts";
const phoneHref = site.business.phoneUri;
const smsHref = site.business.smsUri;

// §39 / doc 30 §20A.6's recognition movement: what stalls a property before it can move forward.
const triggers = [
  "the property is listed or about to be listed for sale",
  "a new tenant is waiting to move in",
  "repairs or renovation cannot start yet",
  "rooms, closets or a garage are not reachable",
  "a tenant or previous owner left contents behind",
  "the property has sat vacant and filled up over time",
  "a family property needs to be cleared before its next step"
] as const;

// Three categories, not five — the operational-scope frame the brief calls for, distinct from
// Hoarding's five-category register and Estate's save-list instructions.
const rules = [
  {
    label: "Stays",
    detail: "approved to remain — furniture, fixtures or contents the owner or tenant is keeping"
  },
  {
    label: "Leaves",
    detail: "approved to be cleared — the contents the scope defines as removable"
  },
  {
    label: "Review",
    detail: "not yet decided — set aside and flagged rather than moved either direction"
  }
] as const;

const authority = [
  "who can approve the scope",
  "who can approve a change once work is underway",
  "who the crew contacts when something is unclear",
  "which areas, if any, are off-limits"
] as const;

// The property's next use. Next-use/readiness frame, not Estate's family-disposition frame — no
// "returned to family" option here; that is Estate's scope.
const goals = [
  { label: "Ready to sell", detail: "cleared so the property can be listed and shown" },
  { label: "Ready to rent", detail: "cleared and cleaned ahead of the next tenant" },
  { label: "Ready for repair", detail: "rooms opened so a contractor or inspector can access them" },
  { label: "Ready for turnover", detail: "cleared to the condition a lease or sale requires" },
  { label: "Usable again", detail: "rooms, paths or storage areas cleared for present use" },
  { label: "Emptied", detail: "approved contents removed, property ready for its next step" }
] as const;

// Field-procedure sequence. §39's six-stage plan, reworded from Estate's family-protection framing
// into a property-progression framing — no "protect important items" opening step, because this
// page's rule system (above) is the scope-setting step, not a save-list.
const process = [
  {
    title: "Define the property goal",
    detail: "What the property needs to become next sets the sequence, the scope and the deadline before anything else is decided."
  },
  {
    title: "Confirm stays, leaves and review",
    detail: "The categories above are agreed in writing with the authorized contact before the crew opens a single room."
  },
  {
    title: "Open work areas",
    detail: "Access comes first — paths, doorways and rooms that are currently blocked get cleared enough to work through the rest of the property."
  },
  {
    title: "Clear approved contents",
    detail: "Work moves room by room against the written scope, not at random and not by whichever area is nearest the door."
  },
  {
    title: "Clean exposed areas, when included",
    detail: "Once a room is open, surfaces in scope are cleaned. Not automatic — written into the plan or it does not happen."
  },
  {
    title: "Review for handoff",
    detail: "The completed scope is walked through against the plan before the property moves to its next stage."
  }
] as const;

const discoveries = [
  "rodent droppings",
  "animal waste",
  "heavy buildup not visible from the doorway",
  "water damage",
  "mold-like growth",
  "sharp objects",
  "human biological material",
  "damaged flooring or walls"
] as const;

const cleaningScope = [
  "floors",
  "baseboards",
  "shelving",
  "cabinets",
  "counters",
  "kitchens",
  "bathrooms",
  "closets",
  "other reachable surfaces"
] as const;

// Phased work — useful for broader cleanouts per the brief, not promised for every project.
const phases = [
  { label: "Phase 1", detail: "open access and clear the priority area" },
  { label: "Phase 2", detail: "clear the remaining approved contents" },
  { label: "Phase 3", detail: "clean exposed surfaces, when included" },
  { label: "Phase 4", detail: "prepare the property for the next vendor or handoff" }
] as const;

const logistics = [
  "access — stairs, parking, elevators, carry distance",
  "whether the property is occupied or vacant",
  "container placement, if a container is part of the job",
  "building or property rules that affect scheduling",
  "other vendors who need access to the same property",
  "your deadline"
] as const;

const goodFit = [
  "several rooms or a whole property need clearing",
  "contents are the reason cleaning, repair, sale or turnover cannot start",
  "not everything in the property is necessarily trash",
  "cleaning may be needed once rooms are cleared",
  "you want the scope agreed in writing before work starts",
  "a simple single-item pickup would not cover what the property actually needs"
] as const;

const poorFit = [
  "only one or two large items need to be picked up",
  "everything is already in one pile and a truck is all that is needed",
  "an estate sale or appraisal is the main thing you need",
  "the main need is construction or repair work",
  "the condition is outside our current scope"
] as const;

const priceDrivers = [
  "property size and how many rooms are affected",
  "amount of contents and how densely the property is filled",
  "sorting and review work required",
  "access — stairs, parking, carry distance",
  "crew size and number of work days",
  "cleaning after clearing, when it is included",
  "disposal and container coordination",
  "conditions that were not visible at the start",
  "your deadline"
] as const;

// §20A.18: 4–7 highest-value, service-specific questions. Answers reconciled from
// doc27ServicePages.ts's `propertyCleanoutsPage.faq` (owner-approved: the hauler-boundary answer
// starts with "No," per that record's 2026-08-21 fix) plus §39's stronger objection set.
const faq = [
  [
    "Is this the same as junk removal?",
    [
      "Not usually. Junk removal works well when everything is already known to be trash.",
      "A property cleanout can also include sorting contents into stays, leaves and review, clearing rooms in a defined order, and cleaning once they are open — more than a pickup covers."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Do you haul everything away yourselves?",
    [
      "No. We are not a hauling company, and nothing leaves the property in our vehicles.",
      "We do the on-site work — sorting, bagging, staging, carrying out and loading approved material — and disposal off the property is handled by a City-authorized hauler engaged for the project."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Does everything have to be sorted before you start?",
    [
      "No. But the more that is already decided — known stays, items approved to leave, who can make the calls — the faster the clearing can move.",
      "Tell us what falls into review before work starts so the crew is not guessing."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Can cleaning be included after clearing?",
    [
      "Yes, when it is written into the scope.",
      "How much a property needs usually cannot be judged until the area is open, which is why clearing and cleaning are scoped as separate phases rather than one assumed package."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "What if you find rodent droppings, animal waste or another condition?",
    [
      "We stop in that area and tell you before going further. We do not quietly fold it into a bigger bill.",
      "If the condition needs handling outside our current scope, we say so rather than guessing at it."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Can you clear only part of the property?",
    [
      "Yes. A cleanout can cover one area, several rooms, or the whole property — the scope is written to match what you actually need cleared.",
      "Larger properties can also be handled in phases when that is the sensible way to run the job."
    ]
  ],
  [
    "Do you need to see the property first?",
    [
      "For most cleanouts, yes. Photos help us start scoping. Larger or fuller properties usually need a walkthrough before a firm scope and price."
    ]
  ]
] as const;

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "Service",
    name: "Property Cleanouts in San Jose",
    serviceType: "Property cleanout",
    provider: { "@id": new URL("/#business", site.urls.site).href },
    areaServed: { "@type": "City", name: "San Jose" }
  },
  {
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: site.urls.home },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 2,
        name: "Complex Property Cleanup",
        item: new URL("/property-clearing/", site.urls.site).href
      },
      {
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: 3,
        name: "Property Cleanouts",
        item: new URL(canonicalPath, site.urls.site).href
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faq.map(([question, answer]) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: question,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: answer.join("\n\n") }
    }))
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath={canonicalPath} schema={schema}>
  {/* ══ 01 · SERVICE HERO ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
      §20A.7: location context → unmistakable service H1 → specialist thesis → service CTA →
      phone as secondary → service-relevant image. */}
  <section class="pc-hero" aria-labelledby="pc-title">
    <div class="pc-hero__media">
      <Image
        src={heroImage}
        alt=""
        layout="full-width"
        widths={srcsetWidths(heroImage, [480, 768, 1024, 1440, 1920])}
        sizes="100vw"
        format="webp"
        quality={74}
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="pc-hero__overlay" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <div class="pc-hero__shell">
      <div class="pc-hero__content">
        <p class="pc-hero__eyebrow">
          Property cleanouts · San Jose · {site.location.serviceArea}
        </p>
        <h1 id="pc-title" class="ac-type-h1-hub">Property Cleanouts</h1>
        <p class="pc-hero__lede">
          When a property has too much left in it to move forward, we help define what stays,
          what can leave, and what needs to happen next.
        </p>
        <p class="pc-hero__thesis">Clear the property so it can move forward.</p>

        <div class="pc-hero__actions">
          <a class="pc-btn pc-btn--light" href={assessmentHref}>{site.offer.secondaryCta}</a>
          {phoneHref && (
            <a class="pc-hero__call" href={phoneHref}>Call {site.business.phone}</a>
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 02 · WHAT IS STOPPING THE PROPERTY FROM MOVING FORWARD ═══════════════════════════════
      This page's condition-recognition movement — Estate deliberately has none; this page needs
      one because the audience is being asked to recognize a stalled property, not a family
      decision already in motion. */}
  <section class="pc-recognize" aria-labelledby="recognize-title">
    <div class="pc-recognize__inner">
      <p class="pc-label">What is holding things up</p>
      <h2 id="recognize-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
        You cannot clean a floor you cannot reach.
      </h2>
      <p class="pc-lede">
        A property can be structurally ready for its next step and still be stuck — because the
        contents inside it have not been addressed. That shows up as:
      </p>
      <ul class="pc-check-list pc-check-list--grid">
        {triggers.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="pc-statement">
        Not all of it is trash. Some of it needs a decision before it needs a truck.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 03 · STAYS / LEAVES / REVIEW + DECISION AUTHORITY ════════════════════════════════════
      This page's signature movement — an operational rule system, not Estate's save-list
      document. Navy field, same placement logic as Hoarding's and Estate's own navy movements,
      different content shape (three-category scope rule, not a five-category disposition register
      or named-item instructions). */}
  <section class="pc-rules" aria-labelledby="rules-title">
    <div class="pc-rules__inner">
      <div class="pc-rules__lead">
        <p class="pc-rules__label">Before the crew opens a room</p>
        <h2 id="rules-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
          The rules need to be clear first.
        </h2>
        <p class="pc-rules__lede">
          A property cleanout is not one big pile to be cleared. Before anything moves, the scope
          sorts the property into three categories:
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="pc-rules__list">
        {rules.map((rule) => (
          <div class="pc-rules__row">
            <dt>{rule.label}</dt>
            <dd>{rule.detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <div class="pc-authority">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">One authorized contact approves the scope.</h3>
        <p>Before work starts, we need to know:</p>
        <ul class="pc-check-list pc-check-list--dark">
          {authority.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="pc-authority__note">
          The crew should not be guessing at any of the four. One clear contact keeps the job
          moving without a mid-project dispute over what was approved.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 04 · PROPERTY GOAL + CLEARING SEQUENCE ═══════════════════════════════════════════════
      This page's second mechanism: what the property needs to become next, then the field
      procedure that gets it there. */}
  <section class="pc-goal" aria-labelledby="goal-title">
    <div class="pc-goal__inner">
      <div class="pc-goal__head">
        <p class="pc-label">The property's next step</p>
        <h2 id="goal-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
          What does the property need to become?
        </h2>
        <p class="pc-lede">
          The next use changes the sequence, the crew, the schedule and the cleaning scope. We
          need to know where the property is going before we plan how to get it there.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="pc-goal__list">
        {goals.map((goal) => (
          <div class="pc-goal__row">
            <dt>{goal.label}</dt>
            <dd>{goal.detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))}
      </dl>

      <div class="pc-process">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-feature">We do not start at random.</h3>
        <ol class="pc-steps">
          {process.map((step, index) => (
            <li class="pc-step">
              <span class="pc-step__index" aria-hidden="true">
                {String(index + 1).padStart(2, "0")}
              </span>
              <div class="pc-step__body">
                <h4 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">{step.title}</h4>
                <p>{step.detail}</p>
              </div>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="pc-phases">
        <h3 class="ac-type-h3-home-route">Larger properties can run in phases.</h3>
        <p>
          Not every project needs this, but a broad cleanout sometimes moves faster when it is
          broken into stages rather than treated as one continuous job:
        </p>
        <div class="pc-phases__list">
          {phases.map((phase) => (
            <div class="pc-phases__item">
              <p class="pc-phases__label">{phase.label}</p>
              <p>{phase.detail}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* ══ 05 · CLEARING + CLEANING + HIDDEN CONDITIONS + NOT A HAULER ══════════════════════════
      §20A.13's boundaries-as-conversion-content, the clearing-vs-cleaning distinction, and the
      disposal/hauling accuracy the brief is most careful about on this specific page. */}
  <section class="pc-clear" aria-labelledby="clear-title">
    <div class="pc-clear__inner">
      <div class="pc-clear__head">
        <p class="pc-label">Clearing, cleaning, and what changes the scope</p>
        <h2 id="clear-title" class="ac-type-h2-home-feature">
          Clearing is not the same as cleaning.
        </h2>
        <p class="pc-lede">
          A room full of furniture, boxes and contents cannot be fully cleaned yet — the floor,
          baseboards and corners underneath are not visible from the doorway. Once approved
          contents are cleared, the areas exposed can be reviewed for cleaning.
        </p>
      </div>

